Subject: [PATCH 57/57] drivers/pci: every probed node gets its probe-addr, so my-space answers

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc unix

`my-space` is the word by which every 1275 PCI card driver locates ITSELF, and
it is `?my-self >in.device-node @ >dn.probe-addr @` -- a field of the DEVICE
NODE. The only writer in the tree is `set-args` (forth/device/other.fs), which
stores through `my-self ihandle>phandle`: the node of the CURRENT INSTANCE.
ob_configure_pci_device() does call set-args, and on x86/amd64 there is no
instance of the node being built at that moment, so the store does not land on
it and probe-addr stays 0.

WHAT THAT COSTS, MEASURED 2026-09-03 on amd64: every e1000 node reads
probe-addr 0, so a card's FCode driver asking `my-space " config-l@"
$call-parent` is handed config address 0 -- bus 0, device 0 -- and reads the
HOST BRIDGE (8086:1237) while believing it read itself. Nothing errors. The
driver simply describes the wrong device, which is the failure mode this repo
keeps naming: a mechanism that looks like it worked.

THE ARCH MATRIX IS WHAT FOUND IT. ppc's nodes come out with probe-addr set
(0x1000 for device 2), so on ppc the card reads its own id with or without this
patch; on x86 and amd64 it does not. One arch answering differently is the
whole reason this lab drives four.

The value stored is the config-space address the node's own `reg` property
already carries (pci_set_reg: phys.hi = config->dev), so this stores exactly
what set-args would have, and ppc keeps the number it already had.

After: probe-addr 0x1800 for device 3 on x86 and amd64, and the config-space
card publishes cfg-id = 0x100e8086 -- itself -- on all three arches.

diff --git a/drivers/pci.c b/drivers/pci.c
index 3c68753..33fc3d2 100644
--- a/drivers/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/pci.c
@@ -1892,6 +1926,31 @@ static phandle_t ob_configure_pci_device(const char* parent_path,
 
     phandle = get_cur_dev();
 
+    /* -- mklab, B.3 Spike 3 (patch 57): MY-SPACE MUST ANSWER --------------------
+     * `my-space` -- the word by which every 1275 PCI card driver locates ITSELF --
+     * is `?my-self >in.device-node @ >dn.probe-addr @`: a field of the DEVICE NODE,
+     * written only by `set-args` (forth/device/other.fs), which stores it through
+     * `my-self ihandle>phandle`. The set-args call a few lines above therefore
+     * lands wherever the CURRENT INSTANCE points -- and during enumeration on x86
+     * and amd64 there is no instance of the node being built, so nothing is stored
+     * and probe-addr stays 0. Measured 2026-09-03 on amd64: every e1000 node reads
+     * probe-addr 0, so a card's FCode driver asking `my-space " config-l@"
+     * $call-parent` is handed config address 0 and reads BUS 0 DEVICE 0 -- it gets
+     * the HOST BRIDGE's 8086:1237 while believing it read itself. Nothing errors;
+     * the driver simply describes the wrong device. (ppc does not have this: its
+     * nodes come out with probe-addr set -- 0x1000 for dev 2 -- which is why the
+     * arch matrix is the thing that found it.)
+     *
+     * The value is the config-space address the `reg` property's first entry
+     * already carries (pci_set_reg: phys.hi = config->dev), so this stores what
+     * set-args would have and ppc keeps the same number it had.
+     */
+    if (!is_host_bridge) {
+        PUSH(config.dev);
+        PUSH(phandle);
+        feval(">dn.probe-addr !");
+    }
+
     switch (class) {
     case PCI_BASE_CLASS_BRIDGE:
         if (subclass != PCI_SUBCLASS_BRIDGE_HOST) {
